- Households move money out of bank deposits and into stablecoins or a CBDC. Banks lose a cheap and stable source of funding along with the settlement balances that go with it, so they have to replace it with more costly funding or cut lending. A CBDC is a Bank of Canada liability with no default risk, so the danger is that in a crisis depositors can flee banks into it instantly. A stablecoin is a private form of IOU(I owe you), so the danger is that the issuer itself can't redeem at par if its reserves are risky or illiquid. That can trigger a run on the issuer and fire sales of its reserve assets.

- Q2
  - Benefit: instant settlement removes the gap during which one side might fail to deliver. Collateral can also be reused much faster, so firms need less idle collateral and cash, and funding gets cheaper. 

  - Risk: because the same bond can be pledged and re-pledged rapidly along chains, leverage builds up quickly. In stress, forced sales spread across the whole system at fast speed, so there is no time buffer for the Bank of Canada to step in. That can turn a liquidity shock into widespread fire sales.
